{% import "tty/core.njk" as tty %}
{#
  The tab strip replaces the old hamburger menu. It scrolls sideways under
  1280px instead of collapsing, which is the design system's compact idiom and
  needs no JavaScript.

  `resume` is a tab: the owner asked for it in the header menu. `academic cv` is
  still not one — it lives in the footer and in the Elsewhere list on the
  homepage.

  A page tab renders only when the build writes its page. The gate is one
  string: `collections.all | join(" ", "url")` is every URL the build writes,
  space delimited, and `indexOf` looks one up. Nunjucks' `join` filter takes an
  attribute name as its third argument.

  `asset: true` opts an item out of that gate, and the resume is the only item
  that carries it. The resume is a PDF that reaches the output through
  `addPassthroughCopy` in .eleventy.js, not a template, so it is absent from
  `collections.all` and the gate would hide its tab forever. The gate stays in
  force for every page tab, which is the case it was written for; check-build.sh
  covers the asset case instead, by reading the tab's own href out of the built
  HTML and asserting a file exists there.

  An asset tab opens in a new tab, the same way a paper row on /preprints/ does.
  `aria-label` is what makes that honest to a screen reader: the accessible name
  keeps the visible label as its first word (WCAG 2.5.3) and adds the two facts
  the visible text cannot carry — that the target is a PDF, and that it opens in
  a new tab. No glyph. The other tabs have none, a marker glyph here would be
  the only mark in the strip and would read as a different kind of control, and
  the design system forbids a glyph carrying meaning no text also carries — the
  accessible name already carries it.

  `aria-current="page"` names exactly one page, so at most one tab may carry it.
  A plain prefix match gives that for free here, because no tab URL is a prefix
  of another tab URL — `/` is compared for equality only, and `/posts/`,
  `/preprints/` and `/contact/` share no prefix. The resume's URL is a file
  path, never a `page.url`, so it can neither become current nor make another
  tab current. Keep that true when adding a tab: a nested tab (`/posts/drafts/`,
  say) would mark two tabs current at once and would need a longest-match rule
  instead.
#}
{% set liveUrls = " " + (collections.all | join(" ", "url")) + " " %}
{% set navItems = [
  { url: "/", label: i18n[locale].nav.about },
  { url: "/posts/", label: i18n[locale].nav.posts },
  { url: "/preprints/", label: i18n[locale].nav.preprints },
  { url: "/contact/", label: i18n[locale].nav.contact },
  { url: "/Ellian_Carlos_Resume.pdf", label: i18n[locale].nav.resume, asset: true }
] %}
<header class="tty-nav">
  <a class="tty-nav__mark" href="/">elliancarlos<span class="tty-caret tty-nav__caret" aria-hidden="true"></span></a>
  <nav class="tty-tabs" aria-label="Main">
    {%- for item in navItems -%}
      {%- if item.asset or liveUrls.indexOf(" " + item.url + " ") >= 0 -%}
      {%- set on = (page.url == item.url) or (item.url != "/" and page.url.indexOf(item.url) == 0) -%}
      <a class="tty-tabs__tab" href="{{ item.url }}"{% if item.asset %} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="{{ item.label }} {{ i18n[locale].nav.opensPdfNewTab }}"{% endif %}{% if on %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ item.label }}</a>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  </nav>
  <span class="tty-spacer"></span>
  {#
    The search control ships `hidden`. /scripts/tty-search.js reveals it, and only
    when an overlay exists to open, so no task ships a visible control that does
    nothing and no page advertises a `/` binding that is not installed. With
    JavaScript off the control stays hidden, and every post stays reachable from
    /posts/, sitemap.xml and feed.xml.

    The glyph is U+2315, the design system's own way of naming a thing: a
    Unicode character in a fallback stack, never an SVG icon and never an emoji.
    `aria-hidden` keeps it out of the accessible name, which the button's
    `aria-label` already carries. Below 430px the key hint hides and the glyph
    stands alone — `/` means nothing on a touch keyboard.
  #}
  <button class="tty-nav__search" type="button" data-tty-search-open hidden
          aria-label="{{ i18n[locale].search.open }}" aria-controls="tty-search" aria-expanded="false">
    <span class="tty-nav__search-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span>
    {{ tty.keyhint(["/"], "search") }}
  </button>
</header>
